description:
Follow-up to UI-17, which left one `Palette` class with 26 named fields and three instances (DARK, SLATE, PAPER) in `Palettes.java`, any of which can be worn by any scene. Two things are left to rethink.

1. Vibrancy. Collapsing every scene's colours onto shared functions made the default look flatter than before UI-17:
- the accent and logo moved from deep IXDAR to RED_PINK_LIGHT so code keywords stay readable on dark;
- default HyperString text went from IXDAR to white;
- the terminal lost its green prose and its command names went link-blue;
- mesh edges went from red to blue-grey; the marked ring and highlights are pink;
- knot colours are fixed per palette instead of a fresh random hue gradient per load;
- the menu backdrop swirl changed.
Find how to get the saturated, vibrant look back inside the one-class design: richer palette instances, more distinct accent/highlight/selected values, or a deliberate vivid palette, judged side by side with screenshots from master before UI-17.

2. Cycle order leaks to consumers. Scenes that need several distinct colours read the `categories` cycle (and others) by position, through index constants that encode the cycle's order: `GLSLParseText.VECTOR_X..W`, `RingTool.DRAFT_GEODESIC/DRAFT_CREASE`, `MeshBooleanScene.BOOLEAN_A/B/BOTH`, `QuadLayoutOverlay.DIRECTION_U/V` and `CONSTRAINT_*`. A consumer must know which slot of a palette's cycle means what, so reordering a palette's cycle silently recolours those scenes. Replace positional reads with something that does not expose the order (named accessors, or a cycle consumers only walk without meaning attached to positions), so palettes can be edited without knowing who reads which slot.

Also seen during UI-17, worth folding in: model overlays that bake colours at upload (QuadLayoutOverlay fills and points, MeshOverlayRuntime point clouds, edge marks, RingRegionTool's runtime, the DslGraphView hotspot label) and cached ColorText/HyperString keep the old palette after a swap.
